WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:01.867
Hallucination.

2
00:00:02.167 --> 00:00:04.870
Models can invent facts that sound perfect.

3
00:00:04.870 --> 00:00:07.700
Let's see why, and how to engineer around it.

4
00:00:08.900 --> 00:00:10.167
Here's the root cause.

5
00:00:10.467 --> 00:00:13.300
A model is trained to continue text plausibly.

6
00:00:13.600 --> 00:00:17.333
So when a question outruns its grounding, it does not stop.

7
00:00:17.333 --> 00:00:19.990
It produces fluent, confident fabrication.

8
00:00:19.990 --> 00:00:22.900
That's the default of an ungrounded generator.

9
00:00:24.100 --> 00:00:25.400
Compare the two paths.

10
00:00:25.700 --> 00:00:27.160
One answers from memory.

11
00:00:27.160 --> 00:00:29.533
The other answers from retrieved facts.

12
00:00:29.833 --> 00:00:31.702
One makes claims you can't check.

13
00:00:31.702 --> 00:00:33.967
The other tags each claim with a source.

14
00:00:34.267 --> 00:00:35.698
One must always answer.

15
00:00:35.698 --> 00:00:37.067
The other may abstain.

16
00:00:37.367 --> 00:00:39.533
Same model, but now it is a system.

17
00:00:40.733 --> 00:00:43.433
There are four mitigations, in order of leverage.

18
00:00:43.733 --> 00:00:46.816
Ground the model by putting the true facts in context.

19
00:00:46.816 --> 00:00:48.300
That's the premise of RAG.

20
00:00:48.600 --> 00:00:49.884
Require citations.

21
00:00:49.884 --> 00:00:52.667
Forced to point, models fabricate less.

22
00:00:52.967 --> 00:00:54.600
Give it permission to abstain.

23
00:00:54.900 --> 00:00:57.700
And measure, because you can't reduce what you don't measure.

24
00:00:58.900 --> 00:01:00.600
Put it together as a pipeline.

25
00:01:00.900 --> 00:01:02.233
A question comes in.

26
00:01:02.533 --> 00:01:04.067
We retrieve the real chunks.

27
00:01:04.367 --> 00:01:06.567
The model answers and cites each claim.

28
00:01:06.867 --> 00:01:10.700
A validator rejects any cited id that was never retrieved.

29
00:01:11.000 --> 00:01:12.663
What passes is the answer.

30
00:01:12.663 --> 00:01:14.133
Otherwise, it abstains.

31
00:01:15.333 --> 00:01:17.333
Here is the prompt that carries all of it.

32
00:01:17.633 --> 00:01:20.033
First, answer using only the sources.

33
00:01:20.333 --> 00:01:23.100
Second, cite the source id after every claim.

34
00:01:23.400 --> 00:01:27.133
Third, an exact sentence to use when the sources don't answer.

35
00:01:28.333 --> 00:01:30.933
Here's how it played out in Mission Control RAG.

36
00:01:31.233 --> 00:01:40.033
A tester asked about a mission that isn't in the corpus, and got three paragraphs citing a beautifully plausible, entirely invented log id.

37
00:01:40.333 --> 00:01:46.967
The team required citations, added an abstain path, and rejected ids not in the retrieval set.

38
00:01:47.267 --> 00:01:51.200
Now the same query says: not found in the retrieved mission logs.

39
00:01:52.400 --> 00:01:53.178
Let's recap.

40
00:01:53.178 --> 00:01:54.863
Models continue plausibly.

41
00:01:54.863 --> 00:01:55.900
They don't know.

42
00:01:56.200 --> 00:01:58.233
Ground answers in retrieved facts.

43
00:01:58.533 --> 00:02:01.800
Require citations, and allow the model to abstain.

44
00:02:02.100 --> 00:02:03.767
Then measure faithfulness.

45
00:02:03.767 --> 00:02:05.433
Next up: function calling.
